+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+The repository has no source yet; `package.json` uses `node --test` with ESM and `tsconfig.json` includes `src`. Later features will import the pointer logic, so it lives in its own module re-exported from `src/index.ts`.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+- Strict RFC 6901 behavior, safe against prototype-property lookups.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+- URI fragment form, mutation operations.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Module split:** `src/pointer.ts` holds the implementation; `src/index.ts` re-exports `resolvePointer`. Keeps room for the next features.
+- **Errors:** throw a `JsonPointerError extends Error`, also exported, to let callers tell pointer failures from other errors. Alternative (plain `Error`) gives callers nothing to match on.
+- **Parse then walk:** split on `/` after validating the leading slash, validate each segment with `/~(?![01])/`-style check, then unescape `~1` before `~0` (the order RFC 6901 requires).
+- **Own-member lookup:** use `Object.hasOwn` so `__proto__`, `constructor` etc. never resolve.
+- **Array index:** accept only `/^(0|[1-9][0-9]*)$/`; `-` and out-of-range throw because they do not refer to an existing value.
+- **Tests:** Node's built-in runner. Since `node --test` runs `.ts` only with type stripping, tests are written as `.ts` and import with `.ts`-compatible specifiers only if the Node version supports it; otherwise they are verified via the TypeScript compiler. Node 22 runs `.ts` natively, so no build step was needed.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Node version lacks type stripping] → add `typescript` as a dev dependency and compile before testing.
